    "question": "Three plastic chairs cost as much as a portable table. Five plastic chairs cost $55. If Manny wants to buy one portable table and two chairs, how much will be left in his $100?",
    "gold": "45",
    "answer": "Each plastic chair costs $55/5 = $<<55/5=11>>11.\nTwo chairs cost $11 x 2 = $<<11*2=22>>22.\nA portable table costs $11 x 3 = $<<11*3=33>>33.\nManny has to pay a total of $22 + $33 = $<<22+33=55>>55.\nThus, Manny will be left with $100 - $55 = $<<100-55=45>>45.\n#### 45"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the cost of one plastic chair and one portable table, and then calculate how much Manny will have left after purchasing one portable table and two chairs.\n\nFirst, let's find the cost of one plastic chair. We know that five plastic chairs cost $55. Therefore, the cost of one plastic chair is:\n\\[\n\\text{Cost of one plastic chair} = \\frac{55}{5} = 11 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the cost of one portable table. We know that three plastic chairs cost as much as one portable table. Since three plastic chairs cost $11, the cost of one portable table is:\n\\[\n\\text{Cost of one portable table} = \\frac{11}{3} \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to calculate the total cost of one portable table and two plastic chairs. The cost of two plastic chairs is:\n\\[\n\\text{Cost of two plastic chairs} = 2 \\times 11 = 22 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\nSo, the total cost of one portable table and two plastic chairs is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total cost} = 11 + 22 = 33 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nManny has $100, so the amount of money he will have left after purchasing one portable table and two chairs is:\n\\[\n\\text{Money left} = 100 - 33 = 67 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{67}\n\\]",
